30
00:03:02,223 --> 00:03:10,329
With housing and the cost of living, it's continuing to price working families out of Massachusetts.

31
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:19,256
Are there any specific state-level policies that you will champion to help lower costs?

32
00:03:19,356 --> 00:03:23,920
I mean, is there something in particular you think you can do or get done

33
00:03:25,362 --> 00:03:28,684
Yeah, I mean, affordability, it's not just one issue, right?

34
00:03:28,844 --> 00:03:29,584
It's housing.

35
00:03:29,604 --> 00:03:30,785
I am a renter.

36
00:03:31,125 --> 00:03:38,169
I'm very aware of how expensive it is here in our communities, how expensive it's gotten.

37
00:03:38,249 --> 00:03:40,890
So housing is one, but then also wages.

38
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:45,433
People's wages haven't kept up with the cost of living, inflation, and how expensive everything is.

39
00:03:46,213 --> 00:03:48,634
I constantly hear from people struggling with groceries.

40
00:03:50,295 --> 00:03:52,496
you know, people who need help getting food.

41
00:03:53,516 --> 00:03:55,597
So it isn't just one thing.

42
00:03:55,757 --> 00:03:58,779
It is energy costs, food costs, housing costs, all of it.

43
00:03:59,519 --> 00:04:00,640
So there's not just one answer.

44
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:10,164
Housing, I think, really is the main one, since that's where the bulk of people's money ends up going, is housing.

45
00:04:11,324 --> 00:04:15,746
So I look at renters, making sure that they're not getting displaced.

46
00:04:15,826 --> 00:04:19,948
I've done, I have created a foreclosure pilot program to make sure that

47
00:04:20,308 --> 00:04:25,434
homeowners when they're facing foreclosure can work with the program before the banks take their houses.

48
00:04:26,595 --> 00:04:34,703
And then at the state house, I passed a law to create accessory dwelling units, which are like granny flats or mother-in-law apartments that people can build in their yards.

49
00:04:35,044 --> 00:04:41,030
So something that they could create for maybe parents or kids who can't afford other places.

50
00:04:42,892 --> 00:04:44,973
And then I do a lot of work on health care costs.

51
00:04:45,033 --> 00:04:48,935
I think health care might be the number two affordability issue that people talk about.

52
00:04:48,955 --> 00:04:53,978
I spent my career as a health care advocate getting people access to health care.

53
00:04:53,998 --> 00:05:11,307
And then at the state level, I have been able to pass a bill to get rid of co-pays on insulin inhalers and heart disease medication, because those are some of the chronic illnesses that people can't afford the prescriptions for and then end up in the hospital or very sick.

54
00:05:12,047 --> 00:05:22,994
And there's a lot more to do, but really focused on looking at what the issues are and then getting laws passed and doing things at the state level that can start to address some of the affordability issues.

55
00:05:23,394 --> 00:05:25,795
Yeah, and like you said, there is quite a few.

56
00:05:26,936 --> 00:05:29,377
I mean, there's so much to work on.

57
00:05:29,517 --> 00:05:32,259
So good answer, Christine.

58
00:05:32,339 --> 00:05:32,739
Thank you.

59
00:05:34,928 --> 00:05:44,314
So, you know, let's get into what is your position, you know, on top of the legislation that's, I guess it's been passed or it's in the bill.

60
00:05:45,115 --> 00:05:46,696
You want to know more about it, so.

61
00:05:47,510 --> 00:05:53,295
Yeah, so this is something that I worked on in housing, or I am working on in housing, and I want to keep working on in the Senate.

62
00:05:54,396 --> 00:06:00,260
It's a TOPA, or Tenant Opportunity to Purchase Act, T-O-P-A.

63
00:06:01,662 --> 00:06:06,506
And this is something that would keep people in existing housing.

64
00:06:06,546 --> 00:06:13,531
So one of the problems, we talk about housing, one of the things we need to do is keep the housing that we have,

65
00:06:14,432 --> 00:06:18,340
you know, not make sure people aren't pushed out of housing that exists in the community.

66
00:06:18,901 --> 00:06:26,295
So what we see happen a lot is a building will go up for sale, a big corporation will buy it, they'll kick everyone out.

67
00:06:26,832 --> 00:06:39,364
they'll usually refurbish it into million dollar condos and that you've lost this usually affordable or more affordable building that people have been living in.

68
00:06:40,024 --> 00:06:48,712
So Topa would, when a building goes up for sale, it would give the tenants an opportunity to band together and buy it.

69
00:06:49,773 --> 00:07:04,765
work with like a non-profit, sometimes a city, but often a non-profit to buy it sort of like a co-op, but it's a way to do affordable home ownership and buy their apartment in the building and then not get kicked out.

70
00:07:05,246 --> 00:07:09,029
And so it's a way to keep people in existing housing, not

71
00:07:10,502 --> 00:07:12,063
You know, build new housing to do it.

72
00:07:12,143 --> 00:07:14,884
It's a lot more affordable and people in their housing.

73
00:07:14,944 --> 00:07:20,646
So this is something that I've been working on and I passed it as an amendment in the House.

74
00:07:20,707 --> 00:07:25,709
So it passed in the House and then Senator Jalen actually passed it in the Senate.

75
00:07:26,189 --> 00:07:29,170
So right now it's in negotiations between the House and Senate.

76
00:07:29,230 --> 00:07:30,631
We're trying to get it passed into law.

77
00:07:31,913 --> 00:07:38,263
You know, I just want to, so it is basically, it's like the right of first refusal, is that part of it?

78
00:07:38,563 --> 00:07:44,052
It's basically right of first refusal, and if they don't, then obviously it gets sold, like it's only for a year.

79
00:07:44,363 --> 00:07:44,683
time.

80
00:07:47,505 --> 00:07:48,906
And it is at market rate.

81
00:07:48,946 --> 00:07:51,928
So it's not like they're not get funding.

82
00:07:53,309 --> 00:07:56,452
But it's allowing them to band together for the mortgage to do that.

83
00:07:57,212 --> 00:08:07,780
So yes, it's a right of first refusal that would just give people an opportunity to stay in the home and also keep the housing that we have, which is a challenge, right?

84
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:09,120
You see what's going on.

85
00:08:09,341 --> 00:08:11,182
Well, I see a lot of the housing in Medford

86
00:08:11,761 --> 00:08:12,841
I've been here all my life.

87
00:08:12,981 --> 00:08:20,084
And even with the houses, I see houses that get bought, and a week later, they're gone.

88
00:08:20,104 --> 00:08:24,345
And it's luxury, whatever you want to call it, mini mansions.

89
00:08:24,685 --> 00:08:26,846
Seems like a lot of that goes on, too, now.

90
00:08:27,566 --> 00:08:27,806
Yeah.

91
00:08:27,886 --> 00:08:29,086
And sometimes it's difficult.

92
00:08:29,186 --> 00:08:29,567
Absolutely.

93
00:08:29,587 --> 00:08:40,050
We're losing what was more affordable housing for family, more family housing that was there, that we end up losing that rather than

94
00:08:41,215 --> 00:08:42,836
you know, building more housing that people could.

95
00:08:43,056 --> 00:08:48,357
So this is a way of keeping that housing and preserving it and people an option that they could stay.

96
00:08:48,578 --> 00:08:49,438
Keep it affordable.

97
00:08:50,998 --> 00:08:51,439
Thank you.

98
00:08:51,539 --> 00:08:53,259
Thank you for explaining that.

99
00:08:53,719 --> 00:08:56,640
Because a lot of people get all confused about it.

100
00:08:56,720 --> 00:09:01,202
So there's a lot of there are so many things we need to do for housing.

101
00:09:01,742 --> 00:09:05,543
And, and it's, yes, there's a lot there.

102
00:09:05,603 --> 00:09:06,984
So that's something that I'm really pushing.

103
00:09:07,284 --> 00:09:07,964
It's a good option.

104
00:09:08,364 --> 00:09:09,545
And there's no one answer.

105
00:09:09,765 --> 00:09:09,925
But

106
00:09:10,621 --> 00:09:11,242
Yeah, that's right.

107
00:09:11,602 --> 00:09:13,043
Yeah, no one answer.

108
